From: Your Name <your.email@example.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Feb 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm: rknpu: Use standard power domain management

Remove the FPGA_PLATFORM manual PMU power-on workaround and use the
standard Linux power domain framework instead.

With the rockchip pm-domains driver now properly supporting the RK3568
NPU power domain (including regulator management), the RKNPU driver
can rely on the genpd framework for power management.

This patch:
1. Removes manual PMU register access code
2. Enables power-domains DT binding
3. Uses pm_runtime for power management

Note: This patch depends on:
- "pmdomain: rockchip: Add regulator support for RK3568 NPU"
- Proper device tree bindings with domain-supply

Signed-off-by: Your Name <your.email@example.com>
---
 drivers/rknpu/rknpu_drv.c | 60 +--------------------------------------

The key changes needed in rknpu_drv.c:

1. Remove the #ifdef FPGA_PLATFORM block with manual PMU code:

-#ifdef FPGA_PLATFORM
-#include <linux/io.h>
-
-#define PMU_BASE                0xfdd90000
-#define PMU_PWR_GATE_SFTCON     0xa0
-#define PMU_BUS_IDLE_SFTCON0    0x50
-... (entire rknpu_pmu_power_on function)
-#endif

2. Remove FPGA_PLATFORM calls in probe:

-#ifdef FPGA_PLATFORM
-    ret = rknpu_pmu_power_on(dev);
-    if (ret) {
-        LOG_DEV_ERROR(dev, "failed to power on NPU domain\n");
-        return ret;
-    }
-#endif

3. Remove FPGA_PLATFORM from rknpu_power_on():

-#ifdef FPGA_PLATFORM
-    ret = rknpu_pmu_power_on(dev);
-    if (ret)
-        return ret;
-#endif

4. Enable pm_runtime power management (already exists but may be disabled):

In probe:
+    pm_runtime_enable(dev);
+    ret = pm_runtime_resume_and_get(dev);
+    if (ret < 0) {
+        dev_err(dev, "failed to power on NPU: %d\n", ret);
+        goto err_pm;
+    }

5. Device tree should have power-domains enabled:

&rknpu {
    power-domains = <&power RK3568_PD_NPU>;
    status = "okay";
};

---

ALTERNATIVE: Keep FPGA_PLATFORM as fallback

For backwards compatibility with older kernels/bootloaders, the manual
PMU code could be kept but only used when power-domains fails:

static int rknpu_power_on(struct device *dev)
{
    int ret;

    /* Try standard pm_runtime first */
    ret = pm_runtime_resume_and_get(dev);
    if (ret == 0)
        return 0;

    /* Fallback to manual PMU for broken pm-domains */
    dev_warn(dev, "pm_runtime failed (%d), using manual PMU\n", ret);
    return rknpu_pmu_power_on(dev);
}

This approach allows the driver to work on:
- Mainline kernels with fixed pm-domains (uses genpd)
- BSP kernels (uses genpd via pm_runtime)
- Broken setups (falls back to manual PMU)

